<< /E (Potential of 1.58-bit LLMs in Handling Long Sequences) /K [ 0 ] /P 84 0 R /Pg 15 0 R /S /H1 /T (Potential of 1.58-bit LLMs in Handling Long Sequences) /Type /StructElem >>
endobj
145 0 obj
<< /E (In the era of Large Language Models \(LLMs\), efficiently managing long data sequences is crucial. Handling long sequences poses a challenge due to high memory usage, especially with Key-Value \(KV\) caches in use. This challenge has limited the expansion of LLM capabilities.) /K [ 1 ] /P 84 0 R /Pg 15 0 R /S /P /Type /StructElem >>
endobj
146 0 obj
<< /E (The innovation of BitNet b1.58, an example of 1.58-bit LLMs, is a significant leap in overcoming this hurdle. By reducing activation size from 16 bits to 8 bits, BitNet b1.58 doubles the context length manageable with the same resources. This advancement allows processing of longer sequences without a proportional increase in memory usage.) /K [ 2 ] /P 84 0 R /Pg 15 0 R /S /P /Type /StructElem >>
endobj
147 0 obj
<< /E (Future optimization potential exists for 1.58-bit LLMs. The ability to compress these activations to 4 bits or lower presents opportunities for more efficient handling of extended sequences. This optimization is crucial to meet the increasing demand for processing extensive data sequences in AI applications, from natural language understanding to complex data analysis.) /K [ 3 ] /P 84 0 R /Pg 15 0 R /S /P /Type /StructElem >>
endobj
148 0 obj
<< /E (The progress in 1.58-bit LLMs like BitNet b1.58 signifies a significant advancement towards supporting long sequences effectively. This not only enhances current LLM capabilities but also paves the way for more robust and adaptable models in the future.) /K [ 4 ] /P 84 0 R /Pg 15 0 R /S /P /Type /StructElem >>
endobj
